@rem Exercises for lesson 03_memory (C track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise c-mem-theory-null
@type theory
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does malloc return when it cannot allocate the memory?
@answer
NULL | a null pointer | null pointer | null
@explain
Always check the result of malloc against NULL before using it.

@exercise c-mem-theory-dangling
@type theory
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What is a pointer to a variable that no longer exists called?
@answer
dangling pointer | a dangling pointer | dangling
@explain
Using a dangling pointer is undefined behavior. Typical causes are returning the address of a local variable and using memory after free.

@exercise c-mem-choice-header
@type choice
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which header declares malloc and free?
@choices
- <stdio.h>
- <string.h>
* <stdlib.h>
- <memory.h>
@explain
malloc, calloc, realloc and free are declared in <stdlib.h>.

@exercise c-mem-choice-stack
@type choice
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Where does the local variable in  int x = 5;  inside a function live?
@choices
* On the stack; it disappears when the function returns
- On the heap; it must be freed
- In global memory for the whole run
- In a register that the program cannot use
@explain
Local variables live on the stack and are released automatically at the end of their scope. Only malloc'ed memory needs free.

@exercise c-mem-choice-leak
@type choice
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
You call malloc inside a loop and call free(p) only once after the loop. What is the result?
@choices
- Everything is freed
- A compile error
* Only the last block is freed; the earlier blocks leak
- Only the first block is freed
@explain
Each iteration overwrites p with a new address, so the earlier addresses are lost and those blocks can no longer be freed. Free inside the loop.

@exercise c-mem-predict-calloc
@type predict
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int *p = calloc(3, sizeof(int));
printf("%d %d %d\n", p[0], p[1], p[2]);
free(p);
@answer
0 0 0
@explain
calloc sets all the memory it allocates to zero (malloc does not).

@exercise c-mem-predict-shadow
@type predict
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int g = 1;

void f() {
    int g = 5;
    g = g + 1;
}

int main() {
    f();
    printf("%d\n", g);
    return 0;
}
@answer
1
@explain
The local g inside f hides the global one, so only the local variable changes.

@exercise c-mem-fill-basic
@type fill
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Allocate an array of numItems integers, check the allocation and release the memory again.
@code
int numItems = 5;
int *arr = ___(numItems * sizeof(int));
if (arr == ___) {
    return 1;
}
arr[0] = 42;
___(arr);
@answer
malloc
NULL
free
@explain
malloc reserves the memory and returns NULL on failure; every successful malloc is matched by exactly one free.

@exercise c-mem-fill-sizeof
@type fill
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Complete the allocation of 8 doubles.
@code
double *d = malloc(8 * ___(double));
@answer
sizeof
@explain
malloc counts bytes, so multiply the number of items by sizeof the item type.

@exercise c-mem-bug-size
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This code should store the numbers 0 to 9 in a dynamic array of 10 ints, but it allocates too little memory. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *arr = malloc(10);
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        arr[i] = i;
    }
    printf("%d\n", arr[9]);
    free(arr);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 5
@answer
int *arr = malloc(10 * sizeof(int));
@explain
malloc(10) gives 10 bytes, not 10 ints. Multiply by sizeof(int).

@exercise c-mem-bug-use-after-free
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Click the line that uses memory it must not use any more.
@code
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));
    *p = 7;
    free(p);
    printf("%d\n", *p);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 8
@explain
After free(p) the memory no longer belongs to the program: reading *p is undefined behavior. Print first, free afterwards.

@exercise c-mem-bug-double-free
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Click the line that is a bug.
@code
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *p = malloc(sizeof(int));
    *p = 7;
    free(p);
    free(p);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@explain
Freeing the same memory twice is undefined behavior. Free once, or set p = NULL after the first free (free(NULL) is safe).
